  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...As of early this morning, a deepening \nNor'easter will continue to lift along the Mid-Atlantic and NE\ncoast tonight. Eventually, the system will weaken and lift NE \naway from the U.S. East Coast early next week.\n\nThe dry airmass should keep most of ENC dry today. The one \nexception, though, is along the NRN OBX. There, increasing CAA \non the western periphery of the Nor'easter should lead to steep \nlow- level lapse rates along the length of the Chesapeake Bay. \nThis plus a favorable trajectory down the length of the Bay \nshould support a band of light showers extending southeast from\neastern VA into NE NC...though little on radar at this time. \n\nDespite winds and seas gradually decreasing today, coastal \nimpacts will continue to plague portions of the OBX. Buoys \ncurrently show wave heights of 10-13ft at 12-13 seconds, and \nthis should continue to lead to wave runup and ocean overwash \nimpacts along vulnerable stretches of the OBX, with a continued \nfocus on Hatteras and Ocracoke Islands. Portions of NC-12 remain\nclosed on both Hatteras and Ocracoke Islands. Adding to the \nimpact of the wave runup and overwash is the already elevated \nastronomical tides ongoing through the weekend. Extended the CF\nWarnings/Advisory and the High Surf Advisory. In addition, as \nthe Nor'easter pulls away from the area, a more moderate westerly\nwind is expected to develop by Sunday, and this may lead to a \nrisk of very minor coastal flooding around Roanoke Island.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...By mid to late next week, a notable upper level\nridge is forecast to build over the Southeastern U.S. This \ncombined with rising low-level thicknesses in southerly flow \nshould support warming temperatures, including the potential for\na period of above normal highs once again. Moisture is forecast\nto be slower to return, and this should support mostly dry \nconditions for much of the upcoming week.",
